<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Settings for `dotnet-coverage collect -s` in ci.yml (backend-test-shard, both collect steps).

  WHAT: instrument ONLY this repository's assemblies. Every project here builds an assembly named
  HealthcareSupport.CaseEvaluation[.X] (no <AssemblyName> override anywhere), so a rule on that file
  name keeps all of our modules, test assemblies included, and nothing else.

  WHY: dotnet-coverage instruments every module that loads and has a PDB. A dependency that ships
  SourceLinked PDBs then lands in the backend report as if it were our code, and the Coverage: Floors
  gate refuses it (#683: "counted file(s) are not tracked in this repository"). That happened with
  FluentValidation, MessagePack and HealthChecks.UI. Each was answered by naming the vendor in
  .coverage-exclusions, one at a time, which that file itself calls the problem rather than the
  solution. This stops dependencies being instrumented at all.

  DEFAULTS ARE KEPT: a custom list MERGES with the built-in default exclusions (mergeDefaults defaults
  to True, since 17.14; CI pins 18.11.0). So nothing the defaults exclude today, for example
  [ExcludeFromCodeCoverage], starts counting. Source: microsoft/codecoverage docs/configuration.md.

  Patterns are case-insensitive ECMAScript regexes over the module's file path.

  KNOWN LIMIT: a future project whose assembly name lacks the prefix would drop out of coverage
  silently. Keep the prefix, or add its name here.
-->
<Configuration>
  <CodeCoverage>
    <ModulePaths>
      <Include>
        <ModulePath>.*[\\/]HealthcareSupport\.CaseEvaluation(\.[^\\/]*)?\.dll$</ModulePath>
      </Include>
    </ModulePaths>
  </CodeCoverage>
</Configuration>
